A||||16||MCordray||Just Wondering...||||5
Z||000000||MCordray||10-30-1999||10:48 PM||||I was talking to a guy at the auto store today & he said that GM no longer makes parts for the Fiero. Can anyone verify this? Is this typical? Just curious.||||reg||5
Z||000001||trackerrrr||10-30-1999||11:10 PM||||That may be true.  I've been told by several GM parts departments that GM stops making parts for a particular car/model 10 years after they are produced.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-30-1999||11:14 PM||||I was just at one of the local Pontiac dealers last weekend.  I don't know if they're Fiero specific, but GM does still make the master and slave cylinders for the v-6 5-speed Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kel||10-31-1999||03:07 AM||||I was looking for a front fascia for my 86 SE and was told by every Pontiac dealership I called that GM did not make them anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||10-31-1999||03:39 AM||||I know sometimes they may have parts still on the shelf at the dealership.  I dont know about body parts, ask if they can look around for you, if some dealer may have it on their shelf with dust on it.  The engine parts are still being made i think, cause GM used the engines in so many cars.  To me its sad when GM or Ford stop making parts for a car that isn't in production.  You have to rely on the junkyard for your parts.  I cry on the inside going to a junkyard seeing all those cars just gone, but whats worse is knowing theres a lot of cars that are crushed.  Id rather have them be dismantled for parts.  I seen the Fiero store have some front bumpers on sale i think.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-31-1999||08:33 AM||||My local dealer does have some stuff on the shelf for my Fiero. But when he doesn't have any parts I need, he has the parts transferred over from Cinci.  Everything I've needed I've been able to get.  He doesn't know how much longer these parts are going to be *made*. Maybe he just says made when he he means left.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||10-31-1999||09:02 AM||||I call my local dealer and I would say that I am able to get over 50% of all the parts I need. Also my local parts house where I have an open account gets me almost everything I need and they are Delco Parts||||reg||10
Z||000007||Greg Piet||10-31-1999||09:56 AM||||What about kick hill?   That fiero graveyard seems to have oodles of parts, i think they would have a fascia||||reg||1
Z||000008||TooLowFiero||10-31-1999||10:34 AM||||another place to get basically everything you need for the fiero is at  <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>they have just about every part you need including fascia's and they get it to you quite quickly also i ordered a steering dampener and got it in three days thru the mail  well good luck<P>TooLow||||reg||1
Z||000009||Boxdin||10-31-1999||11:03 PM||||i just read last week that a company called RareParts is now producing a complete line of front end parts for 88 Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Fiero||10-31-1999||11:10 PM||||That's cool Boxdin...One thing to remember to all the '88 Fiero Owners...The suspension for our cars will be more expensive to replace than the older model fiero's. And reason is being so unique of the design... Expect to pay top dollar for it....||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||10-31-1999||11:30 PM||||I've ordered lots of stuff from the Fiero Store, soon to place order when I replace my clutch and stuff.  They're always polite, answer the phone quickly and are always very helpful.  Never had a problem with them, many times they are even cheaper than the dealer(no surprise there). I buy stuff the Fiero Store doesn't carry from GM, never had a problem with them either, almost always very helpful and friendly(very expensive though). But,only the best for my one and only  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||kel||11-01-1999||01:19 AM||||I'm not looking for a front fascia anymore, but when I was no one seemed to have them.  They heated mine up (it was dented) and that restores the memory of the plastic.  Repainted it and it looks very good.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||11-02-1999||01:25 AM||||Oh Kel,do you happen to know whether your fascia was heated just around the damaged area,or the entire part.<BR>My oven isn't big enough for the whole thing,and I have a smooshed in corner on my car.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||11-02-1999||08:41 PM||||I have used a heat gun (not a hair dryer) plenty of times to repair bent bumper covers. It also helps to let the cover sit out in the sun for a while off the car to get warm and help it "relax". Heat the cover on the inside and outside and while wearing some thick work gloves, push the damaged area back into shape. You will have to get it pretty hot so be careful not to burn yourself. Once back in shape, pour on some cold water to make the plastic take a set. And remember- you can make surface repairs with regular body filler on the bumpers.||||reg||6
Z||000015||kel||11-03-1999||10:10 PM||||lowCG,<BR>I remember them saying that they used a heat gun.  Ed Parks from the Fiero Factory had explained "hot tanking" to me, but the body shops around here had never heard of it.  Basically it does the same thing, just 2 different methods I guess.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||kel||11-03-1999||10:12 PM||||oops....couldn't tell you whether they heated the whole thing or not...sorry||||reg||1
